@page "/"
@layout ContainerlessLayout

<div class="py-5 mb-4 bg-light rounded-3">
    <div class="container py-4">
        <div class="row align-items-center">
            <div class="col-md-8">
                <h1 class="display-4">ZIL Forever</h1>
                <p class="lead">
                    It&rsquo;s scary, ugly, and hard to understand&mdash;and until recently, it was dead.
                    But it&rsquo;s back on its feet again, and you&rsquo;re gonna love it.
                </p>
                <p class="text-muted">
                    Before video games, there was interactive fiction.
                    The genre was defined by classics like <ExternalLink href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Zork">Zork</ExternalLink>,
                    <ExternalLink href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Planetfall">Planetfall</ExternalLink>,
                    and <ExternalLink href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Enchanter_(video_game)">Enchanter</ExternalLink>,
                    which were written in Infocom&rsquo;s custom programming language:
                    the Zork Implementation Language, or ZIL.
                </p>
                <div class="d-flex flex-wrap gap-3 align-items-start mb-3">
                    <a class="btn btn-primary btn-lg" href="about" role="button">Learn more &raquo;</a>
                    <DownloadButton />
                </div>
            </div>
            <div class="col-md-4 text-md-end mt-3 mt-md-0">
                <img src="/sample-zil-code.png" alt="Sample ZIL code" class="rounded" style="max-width:100%;height:auto;" width="464" height="319" />
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>
</div>

<div class="container">
    <div class="row">
        <div class="col-md-4">
            <h2>Portable</h2>
            <p>
                ZILF games run anywhere there&rsquo;s a <ExternalLink href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Z-machine">Z-machine</ExternalLink>
                interpreter, including most computers, phones, and web browsers. ZILF itself is built with
                <ExternalLink href="https://dotnet.microsoft.com/">.NET</ExternalLink> and runs on most computers too&mdash;Windows,
                Linux, and macOS. You can even try it out right here, on the web.
            </p>
            <p><a class="btn btn-secondary" href="project/new" role="button">Try it now &raquo;</a></p>
        </div>
        <div class="col-md-4">
            <h2>Retro</h2>
            <p>
                ZIL is based on <ExternalLink href="https://mdl-language.readthedocs.io/en/latest/">MDL</ExternalLink>, a Lisp-like
                language developed for AI research in the 1970s. And it feels like it. All caps? Check.
                Command line interface? Oh, yeah. Cryptic names? We got &rsquo;em.
            </p>
            <p><a class="btn btn-secondary" href="repl" role="button">Nerd it up &raquo;</a></p>
        </div>
        <div class="col-md-4">
            <h2>Open</h2>
            <p>
                ZILF is free, open source software distributed under the GNU General Public License. You
                can see the code, report issues, and submit patches at the Heptapod project site.
            </p>
            <p><ExternalLink class="btn btn-secondary" href="https://foss.heptapod.net/zilf/zilf/" role="button">View project &raquo;</ExternalLink></p>
        </div>
    </div>

    <hr>

</div> <!-- /container -->
